# NOTES
# 1. GEC turbine default was WRONG for every Magnox-era station in this batch (Berkeley: AEI; Bradwell/Calder Hall/Chapelcross/Dungeness A/Dungeness B: C.A. Parsons & Co.). GEC only became correct once it had absorbed AEI (1968) and English Electric's turbine business (1968-69) — it is confirmed correct for Hartlepool and Heysham A (started construction 1968-70), but WRONG again for Heysham B, which used NEI (Northern Engineering Industries, the 1977 Parsons/Reyrolle-lineage merger) rather than GEC. The heuristic's blanket "GEC for all" default ignores this timeline entirely.
# 2. RPV "(none)" default is confirmed WRONG at Calder Hall (steel vessel by Whessoe) and Dounreay DFR (139-ft steel containment sphere by Motherwell Bridge). It is UNVERIFIABLE (not confirmed either way) for Berkeley, Bradwell, Chapelcross, Dungeness A, and Dounreay PFR — these are all early/non-standard designs where a steel vessel is plausible by era/design analogy but no source names the specific station's vessel material or fabricator. "(none)" is genuinely CONFIRMED only for the later AGR stations (Dungeness B, Hartlepool, Heysham A, Heysham B), which use prestressed-concrete integral pressure vessels by design, not a retrofitted default.
# 3. The heuristic's pattern of copying an identical value into both constructor and architect_engineer is a real risk, not just a formatting quirk: it happened to be correct for stations where one commercial consortium truly did both jobs (Berkeley: AEI-John Thompson; Bradwell: NPPC; Dungeness A: TNPG; Heysham B: NNC), but WRONG where a government design authority and a separate civil contractor were conflated (Calder Hall: constructor was Taylor Woodrow, not UKAEA; Chapelcross: constructor was Mitchell Construction, not UKAEA) or where a consortium reorganization split the two roles over time (Hartlepool and Heysham A: civil construction was BNDC/NDC but reactor design/supply is separately attributed to NNC, formed 1973 mid-construction).
# 4. Dungeness B's constructor/AE was collapsed into a single slashed string "APC/BNDC" by the heuristic, obscuring that these were two SEQUENTIAL contractors (Atomic Power Constructions from 1965, replaced by BNDC after APC's 1969 insolvency), not a joint venture — a materially different fact than the heuristic's formatting implies.
# 5. Dounreay PFR is a near-total sourcing gap: Wikipedia coverage does not name a civil contractor, architect-engineer, turbine supplier, or vessel manufacturer distinct from the owner (UKAEA). All four fields are UNVERIFIABLE; do not treat the heuristic's UKAEA/GEC/None defaults as validated by omission.
# 6. Session WebSearch budget was exhausted during research; findings rely primarily on Wikipedia (station articles) plus Grace's Guide for Calder Hall and Chapelcross specifically. Engineering-timelines.com and additional gracesguide.co.uk pages (Berkeley, Bradwell, Dungeness, Hartlepool, Heysham) could not be accessed (connection errors/404s) and were not cross-checked — a follow-up pass against those sources, plus IAEA INIS/WNA, is recommended before treating MED-confidence items above as fully closed.
